# {{ driver_name }}

Linux platform character device with a validated ioctl userspace API,
generated by [drvforge](https://github.com/drvforge/drvforge).

- **Compatible string:** `{{ compat }}`
- **Device node:** `/dev/{{ module_name }}`
- **Shared API header:** `include/{{ module_name }}.h`
- **Hardware required:** none (works on a PC or QEMU via the DT overlay)

## Userspace API

| Command | Direction | Argument | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `{{ name_upper }}_IOCG_INFO` | `_IOR` | `struct {{ module_name }}_info` | read `{gain, resets}` |
| `{{ name_upper }}_IOCS_GAIN` | `_IOW` | `__u32` | set gain (`0`..`{{ name_upper }}_GAIN_MAX`, else `EINVAL`) |
| `{{ name_upper }}_IOC_RESET` | `_IO` | none | restore default gain, count the reset |

Commands with another ioctl magic return `ENOTTY`.

## Build requirements

```sh
# Debian/Ubuntu
sudo apt install build-essential linux-headers-$(uname -r) device-tree-compiler
```

## Build & test

```sh
make        # build {{ module_name }}.ko
make test   # build, insmod, run the ioctl test client, rmmod
```

## Binding the driver

The driver matches on `{{ compat }}`, so a platform device carrying that
compatible string must exist before it will probe. The overlay in this
directory creates one; there are two ways to apply it.

### Option A — configfs overlay at runtime

Needs `CONFIG_OF_CONFIGFS`, which is **not in mainline** — it ships in heavier
vendor trees such as Raspberry Pi's. On a stock kernel, use Option B.

```sh
dtc -O dtb -o {{ module_name }}-overlay.dtbo -@ {{ module_name }}-overlay.dts
sudo mkdir -p /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/{{ module_name }}
sudo cp {{ module_name }}-overlay.dtbo \
    /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/{{ module_name }}/dtbo
```

### Option B — merge into the QEMU virt DTB before boot

The overlay carries its own `reg`, so merge it into a tree dumped with exactly
the machine parameters you are about to boot with: `-cpu`, `-m` and `-smp` are
baked into the tree.

```sh
qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt,dumpdtb=virt.dtb -cpu cortex-a57 -m 512 -smp 2
dtc -O dtb -o {{ module_name }}-overlay.dtbo -@ {{ module_name }}-overlay.dts
fdtoverlay -i virt.dtb -o virt-{{ module_name }}.dtb {{ module_name }}-overlay.dtbo
qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt -cpu cortex-a57 -m 512 -smp 2 \
    -kernel Image -append "console=ttyAMA0" -dtb virt-{{ module_name }}.dtb
```

After binding:

```sh
ls -l /dev/{{ module_name }}
sudo dmesg | tail
```

## Installing with DKMS

```sh
sudo dkms add .
sudo dkms install {{ module_name }}/1.0.0
```

## Moving into the kernel tree

1. Copy `{{ module_name }}.c` to `drivers/char/` (or the appropriate subsystem
   directory) and the UAPI header to `include/uapi/linux/`.
2. Append `Kconfig` contents and the matching `obj-$(CONFIG_{{ name_upper }})`
   line to the subsystem `Kconfig`/`Makefile`.
3. Run `scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict -g`, add the DT binding document under
   `Documentation/devicetree/bindings/`, and post the series.

